<section id="how-it-works">
  <h2>How It Works</h2>

  <p>The pipeline operates in two stages. Both use standard U-Net architectures and are lightweight enough to run on consumer hardware (&lt;3 GB GPU memory).</p>

  <h3>Stage 1: Sparse 2D &rarr; Dense 2D</h3>

  <p>A user creates sparse 2D annotations on one or a few sections, either manually or using a foundation model (e.g., SAM). A 2D U-Net is trained on these sparse labels to predict dense local shape descriptors (LSDs) and affinities. A masked loss restricts supervision to labeled regions, preventing the network from learning background features from unannotated instances. At inference, the 2D network is applied section-by-section to produce a stack of dense 2D predictions.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${base}/fig2b_sparse2d.png`} alt="Sparse 2D to dense 2D training. 2D images with sparse labels train a 2D U-Net to learn dense 2D LSDs. Background regions of the target 2D LSDs are masked out during loss computation." />
    <figcaption><strong>Fig. 2b.</strong> Sparse 2D to dense 2D training. 2D images with sparse labels are used to train a 2D U-Net to learn dense 2D LSDs. Background regions of the target 2D LSDs, denoted by diagonal gray stripes, are masked out during loss computation. All networks compute a weighted mean-squared-error (MSE) loss between predictions and targets during training.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Stage 2: Stacked 2D &rarr; 3D</h3>

  <p>A lightweight 3D U-Net, pre-trained entirely on synthetic 3D labels, maps the stacked 2D predictions to 3D affinities encoding inter-section connectivity. Because the 3D network receives only stacked 2D predictions as input, it learns z-axis connectivity from geometric priors acquired from synthetic training data, without requiring 3D annotation from the target dataset. Seeded watershed produces the final 3D instance segmentation.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${base}/fig2a_pipeline.png`} alt="2D to 3D inference pipeline. Sections from a 3D image volume are input slice-by-slice to the trained 2D U-Net to generate 2D LSD slices, from which the trained 3D U-Net infers 3D affinities." />
    <figcaption><strong>Fig. 2a.</strong> 2D&rarr;3D inference pipeline. Five example sections shown for simplicity. Sections from a 3D image volume are input slice-by-slice to the trained 2D U-Net to generate 2D LSD slices, from which the trained 3D U-Net infers 3D affinities. LSDs and affinities are RGB encoded for visualization. Seeded watershed generates 3D segmentation from the affinities.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Synthetic 3D Label Generation</h3>

  <p>Synthetic 3D training data is generated on-the-fly during training using three distinct strategies to simulate diverse biological structures: morphological dilation from random seeds, section-wise dilation of speckled binary arrays, and watershed on Gaussian-filtered random peaks. A fourth ensemble strategy combines all three equally.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src={`${base}/fig2d_synthetic.png`} alt="Generation of 3D synthetic data through a series of morphological operations and transformations applied to a random array of noise or foreground voxels." />
    <figcaption><strong>Fig. 2d.</strong> Generation of 3D synthetic data through a series of morphological operations and transformations applied to a random array of noise or foreground voxels. The <a href="https://github.com/funkelab/gunpowder" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gunpowder</a> implementation is available at <a href="https://github.com/ucsdmanorlab/bootstrapper/blob/main/bootstrapper/gp/create_labels.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>create_labels.py</code></a>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

